WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.712
This slide introduces text pattern matching with
PostgreSQL ILIKE ILIKE is similar

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:10.238
to LIKE but it is case-insensitive That means the
pattern can match

00:00:10.262 --> 00:00:14.825
text even if uppercase and lowercase letters are
different This is useful

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.500
when names or other text values were not entered
with perfectly consistent

00:00:18.525 --> 00:00:24.138
capitalization The percent sign is a wildcard It can
represent

00:00:24.162 --> 00:00:28.675
zero or more characters If the pattern is c percent
the text

00:00:28.688 --> 00:00:32.775
must begin with the letter c followed by anything
else If the

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.825
pattern is percent son the text must end with son If
the

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.475
pattern is percent gar percent then gar can appear
anywhere inside the

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.850
text Our query selects MRN and full name from
patients where the

00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:49.288
full name starts with c ignoring case Run the query
and see

00:00:49.312 --> 00:00:53.612
which names match Then change the pattern to another
letter such as

00:00:53.638 --> 00:00:57.938
b percent You can also experiment with a pattern
that searches for

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:01.838
text in the middle of a name Pattern matching is
useful but

00:01:01.862 --> 00:01:05.475
remember that broad patterns can return many rows in
a large database

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.462
In real systems you should still think carefully
about what you are

00:01:09.488 --> 00:01:11.688
searching for and how selective the condition is
